{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>That it may go well, etc.</b> Meaning: the reason that I command you to say 'my sister,' and not that I go further to make the distance greater, so that it be said of him that he is a foreign man to her — <b>that it may go well</b>, etc. — for if he were a foreigner there would be no reason that they should treat him well for her sake. Or perhaps it is for this reason that he was insistent that she say it, and it did not suffice that he say it, for they would not justify his words to benefit him. And according to the plain sense, it did not suffice for him to say it, because he feared that she might say something from which it would be recognized that she is his wife. And His saying <b>and my soul shall live</b> is the ultimate good, for he hopes to keep his soul alive in the famine. And He was precise in saying <b>and my soul shall live</b> and did not say 'and I shall live,' etc., to allude to the verse (Proverbs 13:25): ״צַדִּיק אוֹכֵל לְשׂוֹבַע נַפְשׁוֹ״." "<b>Further,</b> it further intends in the precision of His again doubling the matter — ״בַּעֲבוּרֵךְ״ ('for your sake') and ״בִגְלָלֵךְ״ ('on your account') — for he should have said 'it shall go well with me and my soul shall live on your account' or 'for your sake.' However, he intended two things: the first, that they treat him well for the sake of a good sister, physically; and further, <b>and my soul shall live</b>, etc., after the manner of what our Sages of blessed memory expounded (Berakhot 31b) on the verse (1 Samuel 1:11) ״אִם רָאֹה תִרְאֶה״ — that if He does not remember her, she will make it so that perforce He must see to give her seed, by her going and secluding herself, etc., and being examined as a sotah, and becoming impregnated with seed. So too Abraham reasoned that through the seclusion of the woman with the man when she is innocent, HaShem would remember her with children in a manner similar to that, and that is His saying <b>and my soul shall live</b> through the bestowal of seed <b>on your account</b> — meaning, through the matters that roll forth from you, after the manner of what our Sages of blessed memory expounded (Shabbat 151a) on the verse ״בִּגְלַל הַדָּבָר״, etc. And therefore he separated with the word ״בַּעֲבוּרֵךְ״ to indicate that they are two distinct things. Also His saying <b>and it shall live</b> with the addition of the vav is to say that it is a new detail and is not a branch of 'it shall go well with me,' for they are two aspects of beneficence: one for the body and one for the soul. And this matter was achieved for him in the seclusion in which she secluded herself with Avimelekh and not in this instance with Pharaoh, for the reason that there there was no suspicion because he had banished him from his land; it became known that he had done nothing. Or it is possible that through the mediation of both instances the report was publicized concerning Sarah that she had secluded herself, and through this she was remembered. Then there came to my hand the statement of our Rabbis of blessed memory (Genesis Rabbah 53) and this is their language: Rabbi Yitzḥak said, the Holy One blessed be He said: it is written (Numbers 5:28) ״וְאִם לֹא נִטְמְאָה הָאִשָּׁה וּטְהֹרָה הִיא וְנִקְּתָה וְנִזְרְעָה זָרַע״, and this one who entered the house of Pharaoh and the house of Avimelekh and emerged pure — it is only just that she be remembered — end of his words. And these are our very own words, and understand. Behold, the soul of Abraham lived through children on account of Sarah."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:41:06.689728410Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}